# ⚙️ 2. Core Linux Administration

Linux Administrators spend most of their time managing users, files, software, and running processes.

---

## User Management

Tasks include:

- Create Users
- Delete Users
- Modify Users
- Manage Groups
- Reset Passwords

### Commands

```bash
useradd
adduser
passwd
usermod
groupadd
groupdel
id
whoami
```

---

## File Permissions

Linux permissions protect files and directories from unauthorized access.

Permission Types

```
Read (r)

Write (w)

Execute (x)
```

Permission Categories

```
Owner

Group

Others
```

Example

```bash
chmod 755 file.txt

chmod 644 file.txt

chown user:user file.txt
```

---

## Process Management

Linux runs everything as a process.

Useful Commands

```bash
ps

top

htop

kill

killall

pkill

nice

renice
```

---

## Package Management

Different distributions use different package managers.

| Distribution | Package Manager |
|--------------|----------------|
| Ubuntu | apt |
| Debian | apt |
| RHEL | yum |
| Rocky Linux | dnf |
| AlmaLinux | dnf |

Examples

```bash
sudo apt update

sudo apt install nginx

sudo dnf install httpd

sudo yum install httpd
```

---

# 🌐 3. Networking & Security

Networking is one of the most important responsibilities of a Linux Administrator.

---

## IP Configuration

Commands

```bash
ip a

ip route

hostnamectl

nmcli
```

---

## Network Troubleshooting

Useful Commands

```bash
ping

traceroute

netstat

ss

curl

wget

dig

nslookup
```

---

## SSH

SSH allows secure remote administration of Linux servers.

Commands

```bash
sudo systemctl start ssh

sudo systemctl enable ssh

ssh user@server-ip
```

---

## Firewall

Linux firewalls protect servers from unauthorized access.

Popular Firewalls

- UFW
- Firewalld
- iptables

Examples

```bash
sudo ufw allow 22

sudo ufw enable

sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
```

---

# 📊 4. Monitoring & Maintenance

A Linux Administrator continuously monitors server health.

---

## Monitor System Resources

Commands

```bash
top

htop

free -h

df -h

du -sh

uptime
```

---

## Log Management

Important Log Directory

```
/var/log/
```

Useful Commands

```bash
journalctl

tail

less

cat
```

---

## Backup & Restore

Common Backup Tools

- tar
- rsync
- cp
- snapshots

Example

```bash
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz project/

rsync -av source destination
```

---

## Cron Jobs

Cron automates repetitive tasks.

Example

```bash
crontab -e
```

Example Schedule

```
0 2 * * * backup.sh
```

Runs every day at **2:00 AM**.

---

# 🚀 5. Advanced Administration & Automation

---

## Shell Scripting

Shell scripts automate administrative tasks.

Example

```bash
#!/bin/bash

echo "System Health"

df -h

free -h
```

---

## Storage Management

### LVM

Logical Volume Manager allows flexible storage allocation.

Advantages

- Resize partitions
- Snapshot support
- Flexible storage

---

### RAID

Redundant Array of Independent Disks provides:

- Redundancy
- Performance
- Fault Tolerance

Common RAID Levels

- RAID 0
- RAID 1
- RAID 5
- RAID 6
- RAID 10

---

## Configuration Management

### Ansible

Ansible automates server configuration.

Capabilities

- Package Installation
- User Management
- Configuration Deployment
- Service Management

---

## Virtualization

Popular Hypervisors

- KVM
- VMware
- VirtualBox

Purpose

Run multiple Virtual Machines on a single physical server.

---

## Containers

Popular Container Platforms

- Docker
- Podman

Benefits

- Lightweight
- Fast Deployment
- Application Isolation

---

## High Availability

High Availability ensures services remain operational during failures.

Popular Tools

- Keepalived
- Pacemaker

---

## Load Balancing

Popular Load Balancers

- Nginx
- HAProxy

Purpose

Distribute traffic across multiple servers.

---

## Cloud Platforms

Linux Administrators should understand cloud services.

Popular Providers

- Amazon Web Services (AWS)
- Microsoft Azure
- Google Cloud Platform (GCP)

